    "explanation": "The industry standard and best practice is **Fluent API**.\n\n**Data Annotations** (e.g., `[Table]`, `[Required]`):\n- *Pros:* Quick, easy to read, keeps configuration right next to the property.\n- *Cons:* Violates the Single Responsibility Principle by polluting your clean domain classes with database-specific logic. Cannot handle complex configurations (like composite keys or advanced relationships).\n\n**Fluent API**:\n- *Pros:* Highly powerful. Keeps your entity classes 100% clean and database-agnostic. Supports every feature EF Core has to offer.\n- *Cons:* Slightly steeper learning curve and separates configuration from the model class.",
