    "title": "How would you identify and fix a LINQ query that generates inefficient SQL?",
    "category": "6. Advanced LINQ",
    "language": "csharp",
    "explanation": "1. **Identify:**\n   - Enable EF Core logging in `appsettings.json` (`\"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command\": \"Information\"`).\n   - Look for the N+1 problem (hundreds of identical small queries).\n   - Look for queries missing `WHERE` clauses (table scans).\n   - Look for client-side evaluation warnings.\n2. **Fix:**\n   - N+1: Add `.Include()` or use `.Select()` projection.\n   - Missing WHERE: Ensure your LINQ filters are applied to the `IQueryable` before `.ToList()`.\n   - Client-Side Eval: Remove C#-specific methods (like `string.Format` or custom Regex) from the `.Where()` clause.",
    "example": "You see 500 `SELECT * FROM Posts WHERE BlogId = @p0` in your logs.",
    "code": "// You identify the N+1 problem in your code:\nvar blogs = _context.Blogs.ToList();\nforeach(var b in blogs) { var p = b.Posts.Count; } // Triggers N queries\n\n// You fix it:\nvar blogs = _context.Blogs.Select(b => new { b.Title, PostCount = b.Posts.Count }).ToList();",
    "tip": "Use tools like MiniProfiler or Application Insights to catch slow LINQ queries in production environments.",
    "mistake": "Ignoring the logs. EF Core hides the complexity of SQL, meaning terrible queries are often completely invisible in the C# code.",
    "summary": "Identify by enabling SQL logging to spot N+1 queries, table scans, or client-side evaluation. Fix by utilizing Eager Loading, Projection, and ensuring filters run on the server."
