    "id": 100,
    "title": "How would you optimize Dapper queries and handle connection/resource management?",
    "category": "8. Dapper Advanced",
    "language": "csharp",
    "explanation": "1. **`using` blocks:** Always wrap `IDbConnection` in a `using` block to ensure connections are returned to the connection pool immediately. Connection leaks will crash the server.\n2. **Avoid `SELECT *`:** Only SELECT the specific columns you mapped in your C# class to reduce network traffic.\n3. **Use Async:** Always use the async versions of Dapper methods (`QueryAsync`, `ExecuteAsync`) to prevent thread blocking in ASP.NET Core.\n4. **Command Timeout:** Dapper defaults to a 30-second timeout. For known heavy reporting queries, increase this using the `commandTimeout` parameter.\n5. **Pagination:** Use SQL specific pagination (like `OFFSET` and `FETCH NEXT` in SQL Server) directly in your raw string.",
    "example": "A fully optimized async Dapper call.",
    "code": "public async Task<IEnumerable<UserDto>> GetUsersAsync() {\n    using (var db = new SqlConnection(_connStr)) {\n        // Uses Async, specific columns, and a custom timeout\n        return await db.QueryAsync<UserDto>(\n            \"SELECT Id, Name FROM Users\", \n            commandTimeout: 60\n        );\n    }\n}",
    "tip": "Dapper caches information about your queries (query plan caching) internally. If you build SQL strings dynamically by concatenating raw values (which you shouldn't do anyway), you will destroy Dapper's internal cache and cause memory leaks.",
    "mistake": "Using `Query()` synchronously inside an async ASP.NET Controller.",
    "summary": "Always use `using` blocks to prevent connection leaks, utilize Async methods, explicitly state columns, and manage command timeouts for heavy queries."
